3. SOFTWARE

.ti 3
The following sections, in particular those to do with UNIX,
are required reading in order that you may understand what is going on
both at your 11/40 and at the CYBER.
There are far too many computer operators who do so
on nothing more than an action and reaction basis, with no other
knowledge of what they are doing than a mixture of legend, mythology and 
folklore passed down to them from a variety of obscure sources.
.ti 3
If you
.ig
.ul 1
..
KNOW
what should be happening then you have won half the battle to make
it happen.
.ti 3
The following is what should be happening.

.ig
.ul 1
..
3.1 CYBER

.in 7
.ig
.ul 1
..
3.1.1 Quebec Export/Import (QEI)

.ti 10
Quebec export/import (QEI) is the name of a collection of
programs which run on the CYBER 171.
Part of QEI (1ED) runs in one of the ten PPUs and a second (EI200) runs from time to
time in the CPU.
A couple more (1LS, XSP) sometimes run in other PPUs.
.ti 10
QEI is a successor to normal CDC Export/Import (EI).
.ti 10
Export/Import was written to:

.in 15
.ti 13
1) Handle communications between a number of remote batch
terminals and a 6000 series CDC computer (eg a CYBER 171-18).

.ti 13
2) Schedule jobs submitted from those terminals in the form
of card decks to be run in a similar fashion to jobs
run at the central computer itself.

.ti 13
3) Direct printout resulting from any remote terminal job
to a terminal.

.in 7
.ti 10
QEI was written at Quebec University to provide certain facilities not
provided in EI such as the "Q,x" command to interrogate
KRONOS job queues.
.ti 10
EI (and so QEI) was written to communicate with a hardwired
(non-programmable) hardware CDC USER-200 (200-UT) remote
batch terminal.
.ti 10
Since communication with this device was of necessity on a rather moronic
level, the line protocol (computer-terminal question-answer signalling
convention) used is uninspiring and cumbersome.
.ti 10
As you may have guessed you are not operating a 200-UT terminal (thank god !)
however we are still stuck with the line protocol because this is what QEI
expects.
Your 11/40 is programmed to act like a 200-UT so as to please QEI.

.ig
.ul 1
..
3.1.2 Kronos

.ti 10
You may think this is some of that (greek) mythology i mentioned.
You may even be right but KRONOS is what CDC have called
the CYBER operating system.
.ti 10
Some things in KRONOS you should know about are queues.
A queue is an ordered collection of jobs, usually of a similar type
or stage of completion. KRONOS queues are usually ordered by
an associated priority.
The queues of interest to us are:

.in 15
.ti 13
1) Input queue - All users jobs read at the central site or the batch stations
eventually find their way into this queue.
Their initial priority is assigned depending on such things as central
memory requirements and time limit and incremented by one (aged)
at regular intervals.
.ti 15
Whenever possible a job is selected on a priority basis from the input queue
to be run.

.ti 13
2) Execute queue - This is a quasi queue of the names of active
jobs (those at a KRONOS control point) at present
enjoying a small allocation of CPU time.
.ti 15
No ageing takes place on this queue.

.ti 13
3) Rollout queue - if a job does not run to completion after one period
of CPU time it is rolled out to provide a place
for another job and joins the rollout queue.
.ti 15
Its priority again begins to age and it is rolled back in for some more
CPU time on a priority basis.

.ti 13
4) Output queue - When a job has run to completion (or been aborted)
it joins the output queue. From here it is either
printed at the central site or directed to an appropriate batch terminal.
.ti 15
Its priority continues to age here and printing or transmitting takes place on a priority
basis.

.in 7
.ti 10
Virtually from start to finish during processing a job is in one
queue or another and by following a job through the queues you have
followed it through its entire CYBER processing.

.in 0
.ig
.ul 1
..
3.2 PDP-11

.ti 3
The software detailed in this section is for a disc based batch station
running under UNIX Level 6.

.in 7
.ig
.ul 1
..
3.2.1 Definitions

.in 25
.ti 15
FILE      - A gathering together of data (a flock
of characters ??).
.ti 25
A file has two main distinguishing features being a name and a length.

.ti 15
DIRECTORY - A directory is a file. what makes it a directory
is that the data contained in it is a list of other file names.

.ti 15
PROCESS   - A process is a program running concurrently and
asynchronously with any number of other processes.
.ti 25
A process may be signalled by another process so as to alter its
state or operation.
.ti 25
A process may be created (born), may live (run) and may die
(exit).
.ti 25
Some processes may reproduce by 'forking off' another process as
a child.

.ti 15
SIGNALLING - A process may signal another process to alter
its operation or state.

.ti 15
DAEMON    - A process which monitors changes in the contents of a
directory and acts upon these changes in a predefined way.

.ti 15
DRIVER   - A program which directly communicates with a
piece of hardware. Usually an interrupt service routine, the driver
communicates with the system at large via a device file.

.ti 15
DEVICE FILE - A special file for communicating with a
device driver. Output is written to a device file and input is
read from it.

.ti 15
DATA SEGMENT -  The data segment of a process is defined as that part
of the executable object file containing machine code and/or
initialised data. The segment is RW (read/write).

.ti 15
TEXT SEGMENT - The text segment of a process is defined as that part
of the executable object file containing only pure machine code.
The segment is read only.

.ti 15
SHARED SEGMENTS - A process may use shareable segments (text or data)
concurrently with other processes.
The shared data and/or text occupies the space (memory) once only
for many processes instead of each process having its own copy
of the segment.
Inter-process synchronisation is provided by the system.

.ti 15
SPOOLING  - This term is best explained by an example of how
spooling is used at a batch station under UNIX.
.ti 25
Let us first look at a batch station without spooling (no discs or
memory to support spooling).
.ti 25
When cards are read into the card reader, card images are sent
to the CYBER to be processed.
However, they travel along a line to the CYBER which can only
transfer a maximum of 600 characters per second.
.ti 25
The card reader runs at 600 cards per minute and one need not be a
mathematical genius to compute that this is 800 characters per second.
.ti 25
Since 600 is less than 800 it follows that you must read fewer than
the rated number of cards per second or
start picking the characters up off the floor.
.ti 25
But we have only mentioned the input side of things.
most printers are 400 line per minute jobs.
If we say that the average line contains 30 characters (and this is
conservative), the data rate required to keep the printer going
is 200 characters per second.
.ti 25
Because the communication line to the CYBER is operated
in logical half duplex mode (transmission in only one direction
at a time) you will see that if the printer is active at the same time
as the card reader then things will really bog down.
.ti 25
Now let us look at a disc batch station.
.ti 25
The discs are used to temporarily store card images and printer lines.
this is called spooling.
.ti 25
The card reader reads at its maximum speed because cards are being
spooled to disc at many thousands of characters per second if need be.
.ti 25
The printer runs faster because printout is printed from disc as
fast as the printer can print.
.ti 25
By spooling we overcome the stop-start nature of the data transmissions.
a card deck does not have to go
.ig
.ul 1
..
now
if printout is coming down to be spooled as fast as the line can
take.
Spooled decks may be sent at the maximum line speed if there is
no printout being received.
Both sending cards and receiving printout may go on as fast as
possible while simultaneously another deck is being spooled
and another spooled printout is printing.
.ti 25
The key fact here is that spooling allows you to use the
communications line completely all the time.
.ti 25
In general, spooling removes discontinuities in data transfers
leading to a generally smoother system.

.in 7
.ig
.ul 1
..
3.2.2 UNIX

.ti 10
To quote from 'The UNIX Time-Sharing System'
by Dennis. M. Ritchie and Ken Thompson.
.in 10
.ti 10

"UNIX is a general-purpose, multi-user, interactive operating
system for the Digital Equipment Corporation
PDP 11/40, 11/45 and 11/70 computers."

.in 7
.ti 10
NOW YOU KNOW!!!.
